<Article slug="preparing-for-fence-installation">
  <p>Most fence delays have nothing to do with building the fence. They come from a property line question, a missing HOA approval or a buried line nobody knew about. Work through this list before installation day.</p>

  <h2>1. Know where your property line is</h2>
  <p>The fence has to be on your property, or on the line with your neighbors’ agreement where the city allows it. Municipalities put this on you. Chesterfield Township and Macomb Township both say the homeowner or permit holder is responsible for identifying lot lines. If you’re unsure, your mortgage survey is a good start. Chesterfield asks for one with the permit application.</p>

  <h2>2. Talk to your neighbors</h2>
  <p>It’s courteous, and sometimes it’s required. In Harper Woods, a fence <em>on</em> the lot line needs written consent from all adjacent owners. If an old fence is coming out, agree on who owns it first. St. Clair Shores asks about this on its permit form and won’t determine ownership for you.</p>

  <h2>3. Check HOA or subdivision restrictions</h2>
  <p>Cities and townships don’t enforce private subdivision rules. Macomb Township notes that some subdivisions don’t allow fences at all, even though the township will issue a permit if the ordinance is met. Get HOA approval in writing before scheduling.</p>

  <h2>4. Get the permit</h2>
  <p>Most communities we serve require one. Fees, documents and timelines vary: Macomb Township, for example, runs a grade check and then takes 7–10 working days to issue the permit. See the <a href="/resources/fence-permits-st-clair-shores-macomb-county/">fence permit guide</a> for your city.</p>

  <h2>5. Utility marking (MISS DIG 811)</h2>
  <p>Michigan law requires a MISS DIG 811 request at least <strong>3 business days</strong> before digging. If a contractor is doing the digging, the contractor is responsible for placing that request. Digging has to start within 14 days of the ticket’s start date, and anyone digging within 4 feet of a mark has to hand-dig to find the line.</p>

  <h2>6. Flag your private lines</h2>
  <p>Utilities only mark their own lines. <strong>Private lines aren’t marked</strong>, including:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Underground sprinkler lines and heads</li>
    <li>Invisible / electric dog fence wire</li>
    <li>Power or gas run to a shed, detached garage, pool or grill</li>
    <li>Landscape lighting wire</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Show us where these run during the estimate. A private locating company can mark them for a fee if you’re not sure.</p>

  <h2>7. Clear the fence line</h2>
  <p>Before the crew arrives, move anything along the fence line: planters, play sets, firewood, vehicles parked on a side drive. Trim back branches and shrubs that overhang the line, and keep pets inside on install days.</p>

  <h2>8. After the build</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Some cities, St. Clair Shores and Macomb Township among them, require a final inspection after the fence is built.</li>
    <li>New pressure-treated wood needs time to dry before it takes stain well. Schedule <a href="/services/fence-staining/">staining</a> for later.</li>
    <li>Check gate latches after the first few weeks, while the posts settle.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="source">Sources: <a href="https://www.missdig811.org/homeowners/faq.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">MISS DIG 811 FAQ</a>; municipal documents listed in our <a href="/resources/fence-permits-st-clair-shores-macomb-county/">permit guide</a>.</p>
</Article>
